course: everyday
lesson: 15
title: Weather signs and the 30-30 rule
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Weather often warns you before it turns dangerous: tall dark clouds, a darkening sky, a sudden cool wind and distant rumbles.
explain: Lightning is a giant electric spark; thunder is the sound of the air around it heated so fast that it bursts outward.
explain: Light reaches you almost instantly, but sound travels only about 1 kilometre in 3 seconds (about 1 mile in 5 seconds), so counting from flash to bang tells you how far away the lightning is.
explain: The 30-30 rule: if thunder comes 30 seconds or less after the flash, go inside now, and wait 30 minutes after the last thunder before going out. Today experts say even more simply: when thunder roars, go indoors.
explain: Example: on a beach in Florida, a boy counts 12 seconds from flash to bang — the lightning is about 4 kilometres away, close enough to strike, so his family leaves the beach.
explain: A family in Colorado kept hiking up a peak when they heard thunder; lightning struck nearby and knocked a man in their group to the ground, and he needed hospital care.
explain: A boy in Florida kept swimming in a lake while dark clouds rolled in; lightning hit the water nearby, and lifeguards cleared the lake just in time.
fact: Tall, dark, towering thunderclouds can bring lightning, heavy rain, hail and strong wind.
fact: A sudden cool gust of wind often comes just before a thunderstorm arrives.
fact: Thunder is the sound of air suddenly heated by lightning and expanding very fast.
fact: Light travels so fast that you see lightning almost the moment it happens.
fact: Sound travels about 1 kilometre in 3 seconds, or about 1 mile in 5 seconds.

fact: If thunder comes 30 seconds or less after a flash, go inside a building or a hard-topped car.
fact: Wait 30 minutes after the last thunder before going back outside.
fact: A tall lone tree is a dangerous place to shelter, because lightning often strikes tall objects.
fact: A hard-topped metal car protects you because its metal body carries the lightning around you; the rubber tyres do not.
fact: Lightning can strike the same place more than once; tall towers are struck many times a year.
fact: Never walk or drive into flood water: fast water only 15 centimetres (6 inches) deep can knock a person over.
fact: Lightning can strike more than 10 miles (about 16 kilometres) away from the storm that makes it, even where it is still sunny.
fact: Lightning kills people every year, most of them outdoors near water, on high ground or under tall trees.
exercise: Which clouds warn of a thunderstorm? | Tall, dark, towering clouds | Thin, wispy, high clouds | Small, white, fluffy clouds | A flat, pale grey sheet
exercise: What causes thunder? | Air heated by lightning bursting outward | Two big clouds bumping hard into each other up high | Heavy rain hitting the ground | Strong wind blowing over hills
exercise: Why do you see lightning before you hear its thunder? | Light travels much faster than sound | Sound travels faster than light | Thunder is made after the lightning ends | Your eyes work faster than your ears
exercise: About how far does sound travel in 3 seconds? | About 1 kilometre | About 10 kilometres | About 100 metres | About 1 metre
exercise: You count 30 seconds from flash to thunder. What does the 30-30 rule say? | Go inside now | You are safe to keep playing | Wait for the next flash to decide | Count again until it reaches 60
exercise: After a storm, how long should you wait after the last thunder before going out? | 30 minutes | 30 seconds | 5 minutes | Until the rain stops
exercise: Why is a lone tall tree a bad shelter in a thunderstorm? | Lightning often strikes tall objects | Its leaves catch lightning and pass it down | Its shade makes the storm stronger | Its roots pull electricity out of the ground
exercise: Why is a hard-topped car fairly safe in lightning? | The metal body carries the current around you | The rubber tyres stop the lightning from reaching you | Cars are too low for lightning to hit | Lightning never strikes moving things
exercise: Is it true that lightning never strikes the same place twice? | No, tall towers are struck many times a year | Yes, it never strikes the same place twice | Yes, but only within the same storm | No, it strikes only in places where it has struck before
exercise: What often happens just before a thunderstorm reaches you? | A sudden cool gust of wind | The sky turns bright and clear | The air warms up very quickly | The wind drops for the whole day
exercise: A girl in Arizona sees a road covered by fast brown water. What should her family do? | Turn around and find another way | Drive through quickly before it rises | Walk across together holding hands | Wade in first to check how deep it is
exercise: Why is fast flood water dangerous even when it is shallow? | Moving water pushes very hard and can knock you over | Flood water is always too hot to touch | Shallow water cannot carry anything heavy, so it is harmless | Flood water is so thick that feet stick in it
exercise: You hear thunder while playing football. What should you do? | Go into a building or a hard-topped car | Lie flat on the open field until it passes | Shelter under the goalposts | Keep playing until you actually see lightning
exercise: You count 6 seconds from flash to bang. About how far away is the lightning? | About 2 kilometres | About 6 kilometres | About 20 kilometres | About 200 metres
test: At a picnic in Colorado, Ella counts 15 seconds from a flash to the thunder. Which is true? | The lightning is about 5 km away, so they should go inside | The storm is too far away to be any danger to them at all yet | They should wait until it is closer before moving | They are safe if they stay under a big tree
test: Why should a family caught in lightning stay inside their car? | Its metal body shields them | Its tyres keep them off the ground | Lightning cannot hit cars | Lightning bounces off heavy things
test: If sound travelled as fast as light, what would you notice in a storm? | Thunder and lightning at the same moment | Thunder arriving long before the lightning flash | No thunder at all | Lightning lasting for minutes
test: The last thunder was 10 minutes ago and the sun is coming out. Can you go swimming now? | Not yet; wait until 30 minutes after the last thunder | Yes, because the sun has come out and the storm is over | Yes, as long as you swim quickly | Yes, but only in the shallow end
test: On a hike with no buildings near, a storm is overhead. Which is the safest choice? | Get off high ground and away from tall lone trees | Climb to the top of the hill to watch the storm | Shelter under the tallest tree you can find | Stand still in the middle of an open field
test: Which is NOT a sign of a coming thunderstorm? | Small white fluffy clouds on a calm day | Tall dark clouds building up fast in the afternoon | A sudden cool gust of wind | Distant rumbles of thunder
test: Thunder comes 3 seconds after a flash. What does this mean? | The lightning struck about 1 km away | The lightning struck about 3 km away | The storm is very far away | The storm has already ended
test: Why do storm experts say «When thunder roars, go indoors»? | If you can hear thunder, lightning can reach you | Thunder itself is loud enough to hurt your ears badly | Thunder means that the rain will stop | Thunder shows the storm is moving away
test: A boy in Texas wants to ride his bike through a flooded underpass. What is the right advice? | Turn around, because the water may be deep and fast | Ride fast so the water cannot stop him | Ride through if he can see the bottom | Push the bike through slowly instead
test: flash then counted 9 secs till thunder, how far | About 3 km, or about 2 miles | About 9 km away | About 30 km away | Right overhead
test: thunder 20 secs after the flash, keep playing footy? | No, go inside, it's under 30 seconds | Yes, it's still far away | Yes, until the rain starts | Yes, if you stay low
test: storm, can we hide under that big tree | No, lightning often hits tall trees | Yes, trees keep you dry and safe | Yes, if you hug the trunk | Yes, if it's the tallest one
test: last thunder 10 mins ago, back outside? | Wait until 30 minutes after the last thunder | Yes, 10 minutes is long enough, the storm is over now | Yes, as soon as the rain stops | Yes, once you see blue sky
test: heard thunder but it's still sunny, can we keep playing | No, go indoors, lightning can strike far from the storm | Yes, keep playing until it actually starts to rain properly | Yes, if you stay under a tree | Yes, sunny means it is safe
test: storm's coming, nearest shelter is a big tree | Get into a building or a car, not under the tree | Stand under the tree, it is tall | Lie on the grass next to the tree | Climb the tree to be safe
test: Mia says lightning never hits the same place twice. right? | No, it can and often does | Yes, it always moves somewhere new | Yes, the ground stops it coming back | Yes, unless there is metal
test: my cousin's mate got struck by lightning sheltering under a tree and his trainers melted to his feet | Real lesson: never shelter under a lone tall tree in a storm; the trainers bit is probably exaggerated | Real lesson: rubber trainers attract lightning, so you should always go barefoot in a storm | It proves lightning only strikes boys | It is only gossip, so it proves nothing, and a big tree is still the best shelter from a storm
test: everyone says lightning never strikes the same place twice so stand where it already hit lol | That is a myth; lightning can strike the same place again, and tall towers are struck many times a year | It is true, so a spot that has already been struck is the very safest place to stand in a storm | It is true, but only for trees | It is true, so the old saying keeps you safe, because lightning always looks for somewhere new
test: they're saying Finn's dad drove into the flood and the car floated off, shall i tell everyone | Do not spread it about his family; the lesson for you is never to walk or drive into flood water | Spread it, since floods make good stories and everyone in the class will want to hear it straight away | Spread it, so everyone laughs at his dad | Spread it, but add that the car sank as well, so the story sounds even more exciting
exam: It being given that the interval between a flash of lightning and the ensuing thunder is fifteen seconds, determine the appropriate course of action. | To proceed at once into a building or a hard-topped vehicle, the interval being thirty seconds or less | To remain out of doors, the storm being at such a distance as to lie beyond any possible danger to persons | To shelter beneath the tallest tree in the vicinity | To wait until the interval falls below five seconds before taking any action whatsoever
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the protection afforded by a hard-topped metal car during a thunderstorm? | Its metal body conducts the electrical current around the occupants | Its rubber tyres insulate the occupants entirely from the ground beneath them | Its glass windows repel the lightning | It affords no protection whatsoever
exam: Identify the period which should elapse after the last thunder before a return out of doors. | Thirty minutes | Thirty seconds | Three minutes | Three hours
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning shallow flood water. | Fast-flowing water of a depth of only fifteen centimetres may knock a person over | Flood water of a depth of less than one metre presents no danger to a person on foot | Flood water is hazardous only when it is cold | Only stationary flood water presents any danger
